--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADJUDICATION OF THE FLAGGED SEMANTIC CONSEQUENCE (C5)

Question posed: the pre-fix code auto-paired the first-indexed outflow on the
C5 probe; the fixed code queues both edges. Does that contradict anything
written down? Finding: NO. Specifically --

  a) SPEC (REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md, S2 row): "exact ties go to the review queue as
     ReasonAmbiguous". The fixed behaviour is that clause, applied; the pre-fix
     behaviour is not.
  b) Design doc, docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-16-accounts-transfers-design.md
     lines 147-149: "Unique candidate -> ...  Multiple candidates -> closest
     date wins; exact tie -> suspected queue." The doc never names the SUBJECT
     of "candidate". On the C5 probe the two readings are mutually unsatisfiable
     -- P1 and P2 each have a unique candidate N, but N cannot pair with both --
     so "unique candidate -> pair" has no consistent global solution here, while
     "exact tie -> queue" does (N has two candidates at equal distance). The
     queue is the only order-independent reading. Line 232 of the same doc lists
     "ambiguous tie (two candidates, equal date distance -> queue)" as a required
     test case, with no exception for which leg carries the pattern.
  c) GLOSSARY.md:133-147 says "the unique such candidate auto-pairs". This is the
     closest thing to contrary text, and I checked it carefully. It is silent on
     the subject row too, and it cannot be applied to P1 and P2 simultaneously;
     picking one requires slice position, which is the defect. Not a contradiction.
  d) The package's OWN doc comment at HEAD, which the change carries forward,
     states the intent explicitly (HEAD transfers.go, the removed lines 178-181):
       "ambiguous marks rows a tie has already sent to review. They are
        withdrawn from this pass entirely -- both as subjects and as candidates
        -- because a row whose own counterparty is undecided must not become
        someone else's 'unique' candidate and pair by default."
     The C5 probe is exactly that sentence's case. The new code honours it; HEAD
     only honoured it when the tied row happened to be visited first. This is
     the strongest written evidence, and it points the same way as the change.
     The replacement comment (transfers.go:186-200) restates it.
  e) Existing tests: none contradicts. `git diff --numstat
     internal/services/transfers/transfers_test.go` -> "155 0" -- 155 lines
     added, ZERO deleted, so no pre-existing assertion was weakened, relaxed or
     removed to make the change pass. TestClassify_AmbiguousTieGoesToReview
     (transfers_test.go:144) asserts precisely the C5 shape from the other side
     (pattern leg external, both edges queued ambiguous, Paired==0) and passes
     unchanged. The full suite is green.

